From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Vysotsky <il.mohoff2016@yandex.ru>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 08:35:05 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] GPU pixel shading for software WebRender: GPU variants,
 tiles, Gecko

GPU variants. SWGL's builds of WebRender's shaders lean on its rasterizer:
- SWGL_CLIP_MASK has the rasterizer multiply by the clip mask;
- SWGL_ANTIALIAS has it antialias the edges;
- SWGL_BLEND overrides the blend for text shadows;
- SWGL_CLIP_DIST clips.
Those vertex outputs are wrong for a GPU pixel shader, so clipped text
would have come out unclipped. Each program is now also built as it would
be for a GPU (<shader>_gpuv: no SWGL define), and only that variant has
the 9_3 pixel shader and the packing glue. SWGL runs the variant's vertex
half on the CPU for draws the GPU shades. Uniforms are mirrored to it by
name; uniforms only the variant has get locations of their own. Attribute
bindings are mirrored too. glsl-to-cxx gains bool() constructors and SWGL
gains ivec4 -> vec4, which the GPU dialect uses. YUV programs keep no
variant for now: the colour-matrix code needs matrix operations SWGL's
runtime lacks.

Tiles on the GPU. SwglGpuSetTextureTarget makes an SWGL texture the
valid-rect region of a Direct3D texture the caller owns. SwCompositor::bind
uses it when the native compositor maps a tile with a negative stride; the
data pointer is then the tile's ID3D11Texture2D. Draws, clears and copies
go straight into the tile. ResolveFramebuffer pushes any CPU-drawn content
up instead of reading back. A CPU buffer is allocated only if a CPU draw
needs one. Depth buffers are sized to the tile texture, and depth clears
always happen on the GPU, since depth can neither be uploaded nor read
back.

With a device attached SWGL also:
- reports the device's texture size limit (4096 at 9_3);
- hides GL_ARB_blend_func_extended and the KHR advanced blend extensions,
  so WebRender renders greyscale text and does mix-blend-mode in its own
  shader, both of which the GPU can do.

Gecko. gfx.webrender.software.d3d11.gpu-shading (default false) attaches
the compositor's device to SWGL in RenderCompositorD3D11SWGL::Create. Tiles
are then created as render targets without staging textures, which saves a
megabyte per tile, and Map hands over the texture with stride -1. Before
compositing, CompositorD3D11::ForgetDeviceState clears its bound-target
cache, because SWGL shares the immediate context. Every 300 frames the
counters go to the GeckoW10m log.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
